@@ -8,10 +8,28 @@ message id. The client has no other entry point.
 `KESTREL_TOKEN` must be set. `KESTREL_BASE` defaults to the production endpoint;
 point it at the sandbox to test without paging anyone.
 
+## Duplicate alerts
+
+Several copies of the same alert text arriving within a few seconds are one
+incident. Ack them together; do not open a second response or pull in anyone
+extra.
+
+Retries are not the source. A send that times out or gets a 5xx is attempted
+again (`RETRIES` and `BACKOFF` in `notify/client.py`), and every attempt of one
+send carries the same `Idempotency-Key`, so Kestrel collapses them into a single
+message even when an earlier attempt already reached it.
+
+Duplicates therefore come from above the client: `send()` mints a fresh key per
+call, so a caller that invokes it twice for one alert produces two messages.
+Investigate what called `send()` rather than the client or Kestrel.
+
 ## Failures
 
 A send that fails raises `RuntimeError` once the client has stopped retrying.
-Treat the alert as unsent and page the secondary by hand.
+That means no success response came back — not that the message was undelivered.
+An attempt can reach Kestrel, queue the message, and lose the reply to the
+timeout. Confirm the alert did not land before hand-paging the secondary,
+otherwise the secondary gets it twice.
 
 ## Rate limits
 
